Implement user story AMSD-2041-2: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview — Component Verification & E2E Coverage

## Story Description
Verify that existing content components (ContentstackLink, ContentstackImage, ContentstackTable, ContentstackContentCards) correctly reflect live-preview updates without page reload or publish, and add test coverage.

## Acceptance Criteria
- ContentstackLink re-renders with updated href/title when live-preview pushes a change for its content key
- ContentstackImage re-renders with updated image/alt when live-preview pushes a change for its content key
- ContentstackTable re-renders with updated table data when live-preview pushes a change for its content key
- No full page reload occurs when live-preview content updates are received


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single conduit through which ALL Contentstack content flows into the React tree (236+ components via useContent). It currently accepts only a static defaultContent prop and memoizes it once — there is no mechanism to receive or react to draft/preview content updates from the Contentstack SDK. Enabling live preview requires this provider to (a) initialize the Contentstack Stack with live_preview config, and (b) re-fetch content when the SDK signals a draft change, updating the context value so all consumers re-render with the new data.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the static useMemo with a useState + useEffect that initializes the Contentstack Stack with live_preview enabled (using the Contentstack SDK's live_preview config: { enable: true, preview_token, host }) and polls/subscribes for draft content changes via Stack.livePreviewQuery(). On each change, call setContent with the updated draft data so all useContent consumers re-render. The Stack object from 'services/contentstack' (already used in src/api/utils/contentstack_getPromotions.ts as `import { Stack } from "services/contentstack"`) must also have its initialization updated to pass live_preview config — add live_preview: { enable: true } to the Stack constructor options in that service module.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given the preview page is open in a browser, when a content editor edits and saves an entry in the Contentstack CMS, the updated content appears on the preview page.
- When a content editor saves a change in the CMS, the preview page shows the new content without the tester manually refreshing or reloading the browser.
- Changes saved as a draft in the CMS are visible on the preview page even though the entry has not been published.
- When an editor changes one piece of content in the CMS, only the region of the preview page displaying that content changes; all other regions of the page continue to display their previous content.
- If the preview page stops receiving updates from the CMS, it continues to display the content it last showed rather than going blank, showing an error, or removing content.
- When the preview page is viewed outside of a CMS editing session, it renders the published content exactly as it did before live preview was introduced.

